.TH SET_DELETE 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
set_delete \- Delete an element from a set

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/set.h>

.IB int
.IB set_delete(set_t* set, const void* val, const size_t size);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBset_delete\fR function deletes an element from a set. It takes a set, a pointer to the element to delete, and the size of the element, and removes the element from the set if it is present.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB set
The set from which to delete the element.
.TP
.IB val
A pointer to the element to delete.
.TP
.IB size
The size of the element.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns 1 if the element was successfully deleted from the set, or 0 if the element was not found in the set.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBset_delete\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/set.h>

int main() {
    set_t set;
    char* element = "apple";

    set_init(&set);
    set_insert(&set, element, strlen(element));

    if (set_delete(&set, element, strlen(element)) == 1) {
        printf("Element deleted from set.\n");
    } else {
        printf("Element not found in set.\n");
    }

    set_free(&set);
    return 0;
}
.nef